module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.PGroup
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Sylow
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Nilpotent

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BaerSuzukiWanted
namespace GroupTheory
namespace Defs

/-- `pCore p G` is the largest normal `p`-subgroup of `G` — the supremum of
all normal `p`-subgroups. Classically denoted `O_p(G)`.

This is itself a normal `p`-subgroup of `G`, with no finiteness hypothesis
needed: the family of normal `p`-subgroups is *directed* under `≤` (the
join of two normal `p`-subgroups is again a normal `p`-subgroup, using
`Subgroup.sup_normal` and `IsPGroup.to_sup_of_normal_left`), so by
`Subgroup.mem_iSup_of_directed` every element of the supremum already lies
in one of the summands, and therefore has `p`-power order.
-/
def pCore (p : ℕ) (G : Type*) [Group G] : Subgroup G :=
  sSup {N : Subgroup G | N.Normal ∧ IsPGroup p N}

end Defs
end GroupTheory

namespace GroupTheory

open GroupTheory.Defs

/-!
# Baer–Suzuki theorem

Let `G` be a finite group, `p` a prime, and `x ∈ G`. Then `x` lies in the
`p`-core `O_p(G)` (the largest normal `p`-subgroup) if and only if for every
`g ∈ G`, the subgroup `⟨x, x^g⟩` generated by `x` and one of its conjugates
is a `p`-group.

The forward direction is immediate: `O_p(G)` is a normal `p`-subgroup and
contains every conjugate of any of its elements. The reverse direction is
the deep content — proved by Reinhold Baer (1958) for `p = 2` and by
Michio Suzuki (1965) in general. It is one of the standard tools for
extracting normal `p`-structure from local information about generating
pairs, and is used repeatedly in CFSG-era arguments together with the
Bender method and the signalizer functor theorem.

Note: hypothesising `IsPGroup p ⟨x, x^g⟩` for all `g`, specialised to
`g = 1`, forces `x` itself to be a `p`-element; we therefore do not state
that hypothesis separately.

The definition of `O_p(G)` used here is `Defs.pCore`, the supremum of all
normal `p`-subgroups of `G`.
-/

/- **Baer–Suzuki theorem.** An element `x` of a finite group `G` lies in
the `p`-core `O_p(G)` iff every pair `(x, x^g)` of conjugates generates a
`p`-group. -/
/-- Source: R. Baer, Engelsche Elemente Noetherscher Gruppen, Math. Ann. 133 (1957), 256-270; M. Suzuki, Finite groups in which the centralizer of any element of order 2 is 2-closed, Ann. of Math. (2) 82 (1965), 191-212. Via leanprover/lean-eval@38361be, problem `baer_suzuki`. -/
public theorem_wanted baer_suzuki {G : Type*} [Group G] [Finite G]
    {p : ℕ} [Fact p.Prime] (x : G) :
    x ∈ pCore p G ↔
      ∀ g : G, IsPGroup p
        (Subgroup.closure ({x, g * x * g⁻¹} : Set G))

end GroupTheory

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.BaerSuzukiWanted
